The NHS uses a **stepped care** approach. You are offered the simplest treatment that is likely to help, and moved on to more intensive treatment if you need it.

## Guided self-help

You work through a **CBT-based course** in a book, online or in an app, with regular short sessions with a trained practitioner. This works well for many people with milder symptoms.

## Talking therapies

- **Cognitive behavioural therapy (CBT)** helps you understand how your thoughts, feelings and actions affect each other, and change unhelpful patterns.
- **Behavioural activation** helps you gradually get back into activities that give you a sense of enjoyment or achievement.
- **Counselling** gives you space to talk about what is going on.
- **Interpersonal therapy (IPT)** focuses on relationships and life changes.

In England you can **refer yourself to NHS Talking Therapies**. Sessions may be one to one, in groups, by phone, by video or in person.

## Medicines

If your symptoms are more severe, have lasted a long time, or talking therapy alone has not helped, you may be offered an **antidepressant**, usually an **SSRI** such as sertraline. These can help with both low mood and anxiety.

- it usually takes **2 to 4 weeks** to feel the benefit
- some people have side effects at first, such as feeling sick or more anxious, which often settle
- **do not stop suddenly**. Your GP will help you reduce the dose slowly when it is time to stop

NICE says doctors should treat the **depression first** when anxiety and depression happen together, because this often helps the anxiety too.

## Other things that can help

- **physical activity**, including group exercise programmes
- **mindfulness** courses
- **social prescribing**, where a link worker connects you with local groups and activities

Next, read about [living well with anxiety and depression](/conditions/mental-health/mixed-anxiety-and-depression/living-well/).
